AB  - Organic ultraviolet absorbents (UVAs) are increasingly recognized for their widespread occurrence and adverse ecological impacts, yet their accumulation and health risks in marine mammals remain poorly understood. Our study analyzed the bioaccumulation and potential health risks of 11 UVAs in Indo-Pacific humpback dolphins (n = 73) from the northern South China Sea (NSCS), a global hotspot for UVA contamination, during 2004-2023. All target UVAs were detected in the dolphins, with total concentrations (1342.7 +/- 1007.6 ng/g lw) exceeding those reported in other marine mammals globally. UV-329 and UV-328 emerged as most predominant compounds. Although the concentrations of Sigma 11UVAs and six industrial ultraviolet stabilizers exhibited significant declines over the study period, levels of five ultraviolet filters prevalent in personal care products remained relatively stable. A preliminary risk assessment based on estimated hepatic toxicity thresholds indicates that current levels of UV-328, UV-329, UV-P, UV-531, UV-234, avobenzone (AVO), and 4-methylbenzylidene-camphor (4-MBC) may pose health risks to a portion of the dolphin cohort. Additionally, significant correlations between UV-328, UV-329, UV-P, UV-531 and triiodothyronine levels suggest their potential to modulate the dolphin thyroid endocrine homeostasis. These findings highlight the need for increased attention to UVArelated health risks in cetaceans.
