TI  - Multi-factors monitoring enhances comprehension of the species diversity, structure and zonation patterns of Dafengjiang mangrove wetland in Beibu Gulf
T2  - REGIONAL STUDIES IN MARINE SCIENCE
LA  - English
KW  - Mangrove
KW  - Macrobenthos
KW  - Species diversity
KW  - Zonation patterns
KW  - Sediment
KW  - FORESTS
AB  - Dafengjiang is the primary estuary of the Beibu Gulf and a critical habitat for Sousa chinensis, mangrove ecosystem at Dafengjiang is vital for maintaining regional ecological stability. It is necessary to study the current state of the mangrove ecosystem in this region and comprehend the interplay among its various components. This study established four transects within the typical mangrove region of Dafengjiang across low, medium, and high intertidal zones, and monitored communities, macrobenthos, and physicochemical factors of the sediments. The investigation revealed that there were a limited variety of mangrove species and a low biodiversity of benthic organisms in Dafengjiang mangrove wetland, characterizing with low sediment organic carbon (OC) and total nitrogen (TN). Redundancy analysis (RDA) and Spearman correlation analyses indicated that sediment salinity, pH, and heavy metal exerted a significant influence on the diversity and distribution of mangrove plants, simultaneously influencing the macrobenthos community structure. Thus, future work should be focusing on improving sediment conditions to increase the biodiversity and strengthen the stability of the regional mangrove ecosystem. This study can serve as a guide for the protection and recovery of coastal wetlands.
