TI  - Parameter Selection for Using Permutation Entropy in Cetacean Whistle Detection
T2  - 2025 IEEE UNDERWATER TECHNOLOGY, UT
LA  - English
CP  - 2025 International Symposium on Underwater Technology-UT-Annual
KW  - Permutation Entropy
KW  - Embedding Dimension
KW  - Embedding Delay
KW  - Cetacean
KW  - Whistle Detection
AB  - This study investigates the optimal parameter selection for permutation entropy (PE) in cetacean whistle detection, focusing on the Indo-Pacific humpback dolphin (Sousa chinensis). PE, a statistical measure quantifying time series complexity, has shown promise in signal detection within noisy environments. However, its effectiveness heavily depends on two key parameters: embedding dimension (d) and embedding delay (tau). Through systematic analysis of simulated tonal and broadband signals, we explore how different combinations of d and tau affect detection performance, quantified by Delta H - the difference in PE between signal-plus-noise and noise-only sections. Our findings demonstrate that d = 6 offers an optimal balance between detection accuracy and computational efficiency. For tau, we establish a relationship with sampling frequency (f(s)) and signal frequency (f(0)), where tau = f(s)/(2f(0)) maximizes Delta H. This relationship allows for adaptive parameter selection across various cetacean species and recording conditions. We further validate our approach using simulated broadband signals that mimic the down-sweep characteristics of Indo-Pacific humpback dolphin whistles. This study provides a comprehensive framework for optimizing PE-based cetacean whistle detection, enhancing the sensitivity and reliability of acoustic monitoring techniques in marine mammal research and conservation efforts.
